c/app.css", "arrt/src/arrt/http/static/core/editing.js", "arrt/src/arrt/http/static/screens/walls.js", "arrt/src/arrt/mcp/bindings.py", "arrt/src/arrt/mcp/tools.py", "arrt/src/arrt/programming/display.py", "arrt/tests/browser/test_displays_and_labels.py", "arrt/tests/browser/test_editing_a_wall.py", "arrt/tests/browser/test_hanging_a_theme.py", "arrt/tests/browser/test_names_that_link.py", "arrt/tests/browser/test_scenes_on_walls.py", "arrt/tests/browser/test_the_clients.py", "arrt/tests/browser/test_the_controls.py", "arrt/tests/browser/test_the_walls.py", "arrt/tests/browser/test_walls_last_and_next.py", "arrt/tests/browser/test_walls_lead_with_the_work.py", "arrt/tests/integration/test_renaming_a_wall_surfaces.py", "arrt/tests/unit/test_renaming_a_wall.py"], "files_oracle": [".prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-ui-pass-walls.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/design-direction.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/ia-proposal.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/information-architecture.md", ".prawduct/change-log.md", ".prawduct/operator-verification.md"], "findings": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "The card goes stale after a rotation, shapes or mat change made in the Edit\u2026 dialog", "recommendation": "Instance. `editWall` opens its panel with no `onClose`. The rotation, shapes and mat panels report the change only inside the dialog, which is the right choice while it stays open. Closing the dialog then repaints nothing, so the card keeps what it drew at load: the *Changes* fact (`hangingFacts` \u2192 `playsWords`), and after a shapes change also the next three, the *{n} works can't be shown here\u2026* count and the lead. Concrete case: set the shapes to Portrait so a landscape theme darkens the wall, close the dialog, and the card still says 'Changes every 3 minutes' with its old next three and no excluded works. The old *How {wall} is set up* disclosure kept 'a repaint would close the disclosure' as its reason not to repaint. That reason no longer holds once the panels close as a dialog, and the comments in `rotationPanel`, `shapesPanel` and `matPanel` still give it. Fix: mark the dialog dirty when any panel's act succeeds, and call `refresh()` from `onClose` when it is dirty. Then add a browser test that sets a non-default rotation in the dialog, closes it with Escape, and reads the card's *Changes* fact. No current test reads the card after the dialog closes on one of these three panels.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/http/static/screens/walls.js", "arrt/tests/browser/test_walls_lead_with_the_work.py"], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "warning", "title": "Chunk 04 Done-when 2 was narrowed from 'showing in the suite's Player double' to 'a feed is published', and no record says so", "recommendation": "Instance. Done-when 2 asks that adding a wall with a Player and a theme leaves it 'showing in the suite's Player double, end to end through the feed'. The curation suite has no Player double; I searched tests/ for one and found none. The test that stands for it, `test_a_wall_added_here_can_be_put_on_a_screen_and_hung_and_its_feed_is_published`, asserts only `published_manifest_v2(study.id) is not None` plus the assignment and the hanging. It never checks that the feed names Nighthawks, or that the Player the wall was put on could fetch it. The plan's 04 decision line covers the dialog's shape ('Done-when 2 is met through those two acts') but not this narrowing. Fix: add one Decisions line saying the end-to-end leg is a published feed because Players live in arrt-player, and that chunk 05's operator walk covers the real Player. Then strengthen the assertion to read the published feed and assert it carries the hung work. If a client-facing feed route exists, read the feed through it with the client's credentials (`core.md`: 'When a behaviour matters because something calls or reads it, test through that caller').", "files": ["arrt/tests/browser/test_editing_a_wall.py"], "fid": "R-2"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "note", "title": "Done-when 3 (Cancel and Escape change nothing) is tested only for Escape on Edit\u2026", "recommendation": "Instance. `test_escape_closes_the_dialog_and_changes_nothing` covers Escape on Edit\u2026 with a server read. Two cases have no server-side assertion: *Cancel* on *Add wall\u2026* (that no wall was created), and the Edit dialog's *Close* button, which is that dialog's Cancel. Both are cheap to add, and both close paths share `openPanel`, so the risk is low. Accepting this as is would be reasonable.", "files": ["arrt/tests/browser/test_editing_a_wall.py"], "fid": "R-3"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "note", "title": "The Label panel offers label outputs that caption another wall, which the server then refuses", "recommendation": "Instance. `labelsSection` filters `client.label_outputs` only by `!captioning.has(label_id)`, so a panel already captioning another wall is offered as if it were free. On *Caption it*, `ClientService.add_label` refuses it with \"\u2026already captions {other}. Take it off {other} first\". The refusal is in words and nothing goes wrong, but the picker offers a choice that cannot succeed. Filtering on `label.wall_id == null` would offer only the free panels. If panels on other walls stay listed, saying which wall each captions would warn the owner before the refusal does. No edit is required if this is ruled acceptable.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/http/static/screens/walls.js"], "fid": "R-4"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "A dark wall's choice of lead work is decided twice, and the two copies disagree when the next work cannot be read", "recommendation": "Instance. `darkLead` leads with the first upcoming work only when `first && first.work`. `nextUp` re-derives the same decision as `ledWithNext = dark && !now.workId && !now.last` and drops `next[0]` whatever happened to it. When the first upcoming work's read fails (`workOf` returns `{failure}`), the card shows 'Off since \u2026' with no figure, and the list is titled 'Then' and starts at the second work. The failed work and its failure sentence, which `nextUp` would otherwise render, disappear from the card. Fix: decide the lead once (in `nowOn`, or as a value `nowShowing` returns) and pass it to `nextUp`, so the list skips exactly the work the lead drew. Pin it with a browser case where the first upcoming work's read fails on a dark wall with an empty log.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/http/static/screens/walls.js"], "fid": "R-5"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "assignmentLine keeps two unreachable branches, and its doc comment describes them", "recommendation": "Instance. Chunk 04 added `if ((wall.display && wall.display.fault) || !wall.client_id) return null;` at the top of `assignmentLine`. The function's next two branches, the fault line and 'No Player shows this wall \u2026 Assign it in Settings \u203a Clients', can no longer run. They are near-copies of `assignmentProblem`, which now owns those two states on the card. The doc comment still says the unassigned case 'is said, with the way to Settings \u203a Clients'. A maintainer editing the dialog's wording will edit dead code, or will keep two copies of the fault markup in step. Delete both branches and the comment's sentences about them, keeping the early return and its one-line reason.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/http/static/screens/walls.js"], "fid": "R-6"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "note", "title": "Two walls.js comments are out of date after chunk 04", "recommendation": "Instance; prose that nothing reads, so this asks for no fix commit of its own. Fold it into the next commit that touches walls.js. (1) The file header says the toolbar holds '*Scene\u2026*', but it now also holds *Add wall\u2026*. Delete the list or make it relational ('the page's acts'). (2) `assignmentProblem`'s comment says 'Where a wall is assigned is Settings \u203a Clients until its *Edit\u2026* dialog holds it', but the Edit\u2026 dialog holds it as of this commit. Delete the clause (the plan's 'Not in this plan' records the two homes).", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/http/static/screens/walls.js"], "fid": "R-7"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "note", "title": "Two ways to close a panel in one file: a global DOM query and the handle openPanel returns", "recommendation": "Instance; borderline. `askWhere` closes its dialog with the `close` handle that `openPanel` returns. `closeAndRefresh` finds 'the' panel with `document.querySelector(\"dialog.panel-dialog[open]\")`. Its comment says this is because `currentTarget` is gone by the time the act settles, but a captured panel handle avoids that, as `askWhere` shows. The query is correct only while at most one panel-dialog is ever open. Consider passing the panel handle into the Edit\u2026 and Add wall\u2026 sections so every caller closes the panel it opened.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/http/static/screens/walls.js", "arrt/src/arrt/http/static/core/editing.js"], "fid": "R-8"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "note", "title": "The rename route takes CreateWall as its body, and that model's docstring says it is for recording a wall", "recommendation": "Instance. `POST /api/walls/{wall_id}` (rename) declares `body: CreateWall`, whose docstring is 'Everything needed to record a wall'. The sibling `NameClient` is documented as 'to record it or to rename it'. Either rename the model to `NameWall` with a docstring covering both uses, matching `NameClient`, or widen CreateWall's docstring. The wire schema is unchanged either way.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/http/api.py"], "fid": "R-9"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "warning", "title": "The Edit\u2026 dialog's screen and label pickers show port names (hdmi-a-1, epd-0), against a disposition recorded as 'conforms'", "recommendation": "The plan's governed_by disposition reads '\u00a7 Vocabulary, the words for walls (2026-10-27) \u2192 conforms for Walls \u2026 Player, screen, label panel'. information-architecture.md \u00a7 Vocabulary says a screen is 'HDMI 1, not hdmi-a-1'. Before this change, the Walls tables showed the port only with ?debug. But whereSection in walls.js renders `${output.name}` as each screen option, so the owner reads 'hdmi-a-1 \u00b7 nothing connected'. labelsSection renders `${output} on ${client.name}` ('epd-0 on Hall Pi'). test_editing_a_wall.py:90 pins the raw names as the option text. No mid-build decision records this departure. As a result, the owner meets plumbing words in the dialog this plan built, and the new operator-verification step ('put it on HDMI 2 of Living room Pi') names an option the picker never shows. Do one of two things. Either show the screen's display name, keeping the port as the option value, and change the test to assert that wording. Or add a mid-build decision deferring screen naming to #390 and make the disposition say so. Scope: class. The members are every place in screens/walls.js that renders output.name or a label's output as visible text; clients.js keeps its old words until #390, by the vocabulary's own transition clause.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/http/static/screens/walls.js", "arrt/tests/browser/test_editing_a_wall.py"], "fid": "R-10"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "note", "title": "addWallOpener's comment says the dialog asks where the wall shows; it asks only the name", "recommendation": "The comment above addWallOpener reads '*Add wall\u2026*: a name, and where it shows if that is known now'. Mid-build decision 04 says 'Add wall\u2026 asks only the name', and the code builds one name field. A maintainer reading the comment would look for a Player picker that does not exist. Delete the clause 'and where it shows if that is known now'. Scope: instance.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/http/static/screens/walls.js"], "fid": "R-11"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "note", "title": "test_walls_lead_with_the_work.py's module docstring still describes the retired card", "recommendation": "This file was rewritten in this change, but its module docstring still says the card shows 'what the wall draws from and \"until changed\", then Skip, *Not this one again* and Change'. Ruling 2 retired *Not this one again* and ruling 3 retired 'until changed' and Change, and the tests below assert their absence. This is prose that nothing reads. Fix it by making the docstring point to information-architecture.md's Walls row rather than restating the card. The rule that applies is core.md 'When you retire, amend, descope or close anything, first grep the whole repo (no --include) for its nouns and OLD wording'. Scope: instance.", "files": ["arrt/tests/browser/test_walls_lead_with_the_work.py"], "fid": "R-12"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "Mid-build decisions, for the owner", "recommendation": "Scope: none. The plan recorded nine decisions while building, each with what settled it. (02) Rename is POST /walls/{id} rather than PATCH, to match POST /clients/{id}; api.py's own POST-not-PATCH convention agrees. (02) The art_display status and add_label descriptions were shortened to fit DESCRIPTION_BUDGET_BYTES. (02, after review) A taken name is refused in words a person reads, by add and rename alike; the UNIQUE constraint still holds the rule. (03) core/editing.js landed a chunk early because three dialogs needed it. (03) Player and screen moved into an interim disclosure. (03) The server keeps not-again and client as contract names. (03) Edit\u2026 and Add wall\u2026 were deferred to 04 so no button did nothing in the meantime. (04) Add wall\u2026 asks only the name; this changes how Done-when 2 is met, and the plan records that. (04) The disclosure was folded into the Edit\u2026 dialog. There is also one re-ruling: ruling 6, from the next work to the last shown, after the